# #DATA — convert evidence into controlled datasets

**Contract:** `dot-research-data/1`  
**Requires:** reviewed `#INTERNET` records under an approved `#PROJECT`  
**Purpose:** transform evidence into paired, reviewable CSV and JSON release
artifacts before filling the reusable `#TEMPLATE` structures.

## DATA-001 — Intake and applicable-file decision

Start from the current Project Control Packet, reviewed research records,
acceptance matrix, and declared delivery authorization. Inventory the candidate
inputs by stable ID and version; reject excerpts presented as complete files,
unreviewed advisory output presented as evidence, inherited permissions, and
unresolved instruction conflicts.

Select the smallest set of basenames whose row grains are required by the
project. The catalog is an allowlist, not a requirement to manufacture empty
tables or fragment every attribute. For each selected basename, record its
purpose, “one row represents” definition, fields, logical types, primary and
foreign keys, relationships, null rules, ordering, provenance, applicability,
review owner, and release dependency. Produce both `.csv` and `.json`; produce
neither form for an inapplicable basename.

## DATA-002 — Paired-file catalog

`#DATA` owns the following 22 unique basenames and their selection rules.
`#TEMPLATE` supplies fillable records but does not create a second catalog.

| Order | Basename | One row represents | Primary key | Applicable when |
|---:|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | `datasets` | one enduring dataset | `dataset_id` | a dataset is being released or refreshed |
| 2 | `releases` | one immutable dataset snapshot | `release_id` | a dataset release exists |
| 3 | `sources` | one source identity or publication system | `source_id` | substantive evidence has a source |
| 4 | `entities` | one resolved real-world subject | `entity_id` | research tracks entities or stable subjects |
| 5 | `geographies` | one resolved geographic unit | `geography_id` | geography affects meaning, scope, or filtering |
| 6 | `categories` | one controlled classification value | `category_id` | reusable classifications exist |
| 7 | `aliases` | one source-specific label or identifier for an entity | `alias_id` | names or external identifiers require resolution |
| 8 | `entity_relationships` | one typed, optionally dated relation between two entities | `entity_relationship_id` | hierarchy, membership, succession, or shared operation exists |
| 9 | `category_assignments` | one optionally dated entity-category association | `category_assignment_id` | entities may hold classifications by source or time |
| 10 | `documents` | one enduring intellectual document | `document_id` | documentary evidence is used |
| 11 | `document_versions` | one identifiable edition or version of a document | `document_version_id` | a document has a cited or reviewed representation |
| 12 | `url_occurrences` | one observed URL for one document version or source resource | `url_occurrence_id` | an online location is discovered or generated |
| 13 | `access_events` | one observed URL-state event at a time | `access_event_id` | discovery, return, open, review, citation, block, or generation is logged |
| 14 | `source_assertions` | one source-specific assertion or source row | `source_assertion_id` | a source supports substantive content |
| 15 | `observations` | one measured value at its exact subject, population, place, period, and assertion grain | `observation_id` | quantitative or structured facts exist |
| 16 | `claims` | one precisely scoped textual proposition | `claim_id` | qualitative or synthesized propositions exist |
| 17 | `evidence_links` | one record-to-document-version evidence location and role | `evidence_link_id` | an assertion, observation, or claim needs traceable support |
| 18 | `review_queue` | one unresolved issue or required decision | `review_item_id` | unresolved or reviewable items exist, including an empty released queue when required |
| 19 | `corrections` | one approved correction first applied in a release | `correction_id` | a release packet records corrections, including an empty log when required |
| 20 | `release_changes` | one row-level comparison between two releases | `release_change_id` | a refresh or corrected successor is compared with a prior release |
| 21 | `validation_results` | one validation check result for a release and optional file | `validation_result_id` | release validation is recorded |
| 22 | `manifest` | one physical file included or expected in a release | `manifest_file_id` | a release packet inventories its artifacts |

## DATA-003 — Grain, keys, and cardinality

Freeze each selected file's grain before defining fields. Primary keys are
nonnull and unique; composite uniqueness rules are explicit; foreign keys name
one target file and field. A nullable foreign key must have a companion reason
or status that explains why the relationship is absent. Do not store IDs from
unrelated tables in one undeclared polymorphic field.

State and validate every one-to-one, one-to-many, and many-to-many relation.
Use bridge records for categories and entity relations rather than packed lists
or repeated child columns. Temporal relationships carry a precisely defined
start, end, as-of value, or declared unknown precision as applicable. Shared
sites, multiple operators, renames, splits, mergers, and successors must not be
forced into permanent one-to-many assumptions.

Place a value only at the grain it measures. Keep a parent total at the parent
grain, and never copy it onto every child. When a Fact is not entity-centered,
declare a stable subject key or a dedicated observation family rather than
overloading `entity_id`.

## DATA-004 — Stable identity and entity resolution

Use nonsemantic string identifiers in both formats, preserve leading zeros,
and never recycle a retired ID. Keep raw labels and external identifiers in
`aliases`; do not merge solely on case-folded or normalized names. Record the
match method, evidence, confidence, resolution status, reviewer type, and any
superseding or split identity.

Distinguish legal organization, fiscal or reporting unit, operator, site,
program, service area, geography, and other project-defined subject types.
Low-confidence matches and material duplicates remain in `review_queue` until
accepted. An unresolved identity may retain a provisional stable key without
silently becoming an accepted entity.

## DATA-005 — Provenance layers and #FACT storage

Keep these layers separately addressable: source identity; enduring document;
document version; URL occurrence; access event; source assertion; observation;
claim; and evidence link. One document may have many versions, one version may
occur at many URLs, and one URL may have many observed events. Mirrors,
redirects, archives, and canonical locations are not collapsed without
evidence.

Use `GENERATED`, `DISCOVERED`, `RETURNED`, `OPENED`, `REVIEWED`, `CITED`, and
`BLOCKED` only for the corresponding observed URL or access state. A generated
URL is not an executed query; a returned link is not opened content; an opened
document is not automatically reviewed; and review does not by itself make a
source cited. Unknown historical publication, discovery, access, or review
times remain null with a reason instead of receiving the current timestamp.

`#FACT` remains the human-facing atomic research concept. Its released storage
is an `observation` or `claim` linked to its `source_assertions` and exact
`evidence_links`; it is not a second denormalized fact table. Evidence links
use explicit nullable `source_assertion_id`, `observation_id`, and `claim_id`
columns with exactly one target set, plus a `document_version_id`, locator, and
evidence role.

## DATA-006 — Measurements, derivation, and missingness

Store `OBSERVED`, `DERIVED`, and `INFERRED` as derivation classes separate from
verification status, confidence, and reviewer type. Preserve a source value,
label, unit, scale, currency, denominator, scope, basis, and precision before
adding any standardized value. A derived value identifies its inputs and
transformation; an inference states its rationale, uncertainty, and required
review.

Null means no value is present. Declare controlled missing reasons, including
`not_reported`, `not_applicable`, `not_available`, `inaccessible_source`,
`outside_scope`, `suppressed`, `conflicting_sources`, `unresolved_entity`,
`not_yet_reviewed`, and `unknown_historical_time`. Never substitute zero,
false, an empty string, a sentinel, or a source token such as “N/A” for null;
retain meaningful source tokens in a separate raw-value field.

Represent event/effective, period start/end, as-of, publication/update,
access, analysis-cutoff, and release time separately. Record date precision and
fiscal, academic, calendar, quarter, rolling, or custom period type without
inventing a month or day. Document decimal scale, rounding, comparison method,
and whether the logical value is a decimal number or a precision-preserving
numeric string.

For mixed-age, mixed-period, mixed-scope, or joint-service values, retain
eligible and ineligible components, allocation driver, formula, residual, and
conditional bounds. Do not invent a central estimate from an unknown share.
For interentity payments, preserve payer, nullable recipient plus reason,
amount, period, accounting basis, reporting perimeter, reversals or netting,
and recipient-resolution status. Keep consolidation, funding flow,
final-service expenditure, and geographic attribution as separate views.

## DATA-007 — CSV and JSON semantic parity

Every selected basename produces `<basename>.csv` and `<basename>.json` from
one logical record set. CSV contains one header and one logical row per record;
JSON contains one top-level array with one flat object per CSV row. Both forms
use identical field names, field sets, primary-key values, logical values, and
null meanings. Every JSON object includes nullable keys with `null`; the CSV
counterpart uses an empty cell only where the dictionary declares null.

The data dictionary declares each field as string, identifier, integer,
decimal, precision-preserving numeric string, boolean, calendar date, partial
date/reporting period, timestamp, URL, or enum. IDs and postal codes are
strings. Booleans are lowercase `true`/`false` in CSV and native booleans in
JSON. Dates and timestamps use declared canonical forms. Decimal comparison is
performed with decimal semantics, never binary-float approximation; scale,
rounding, and raw precision are documented.

Use stable deterministic row ordering in both files for review, while treating
keys rather than position as identity. CSV header order and JSON key order
follow the dictionary. Document Unicode normalization used for matching and
preserve meaningful original text.

## DATA-008 — Validation, acceptance scenarios, and review

Before release, record structural, parity, primary/composite-key, foreign-key,
cardinality, deduplication, hierarchy, period, unit, provenance, evidence,
missingness, count-reconciliation, and source-lineage results. Spot-check every
high-impact row, inference, correction, manual transcription, and low-confidence
identity plus a declared sample across sources, periods, and entity types.

Model-assisted review and human review are separate reviewer types with their
own identity, time, scope, and evidence. A model cannot accept a material
exception merely by labeling it reviewed. Each result is `PASS`, `FAIL`,
`WARNING`, `NOT_APPLICABLE`, `BLOCKED`, or `ACCEPTED_EXCEPTION`; only the
designated human may accept an exception classified as material or blocking.

The release validation set must exercise these topic-neutral cases:

| Scenario ID | Required representation and expected result |
|---|---|
| `blocked-source` | A blocked access event remains uncited and does not create reviewed evidence. |
| `discovered-unreviewed-url` | A discovered URL occurrence has no opened, reviewed, or cited state unless later events establish each one. |
| `temporal-many-to-many` | Two entities may share a dated relation without collapsing either identity or losing the period. |
| `nullable-foreign-key` | A nullable foreign key stays null with a controlled reason and does not fail referential integrity. |
| `mixed-age-allocation` | An unknown eligible share yields disclosed bounds or scenarios and no invented central estimate. |
| `unresolved-interentity-payment` | The payer and payment remain recorded while the recipient ID is null with `unresolved_entity`. |
| `human-model-review` | Human and model-assisted review events remain distinguishable; only authorized human acceptance closes a material gate. |
| `post-release-correction` | A correction after acceptance creates a successor release and leaves the accepted snapshot unchanged. |

## DATA-009 — Immutable release, correction, and refresh

Assign a stable dataset ID and unique release ID. The release record and
manifest state cutoffs, dictionary and format versions, file hashes, row
counts, status, known gaps, validation outcome, and acceptance identity. A
candidate becomes accepted only after blocking checks pass and the authorized
human approves the packet and documented exceptions.

Never mutate or overwrite an accepted release. A correction or refresh creates
a new immutable release, preserves prior artifacts, records affected file,
row, field, old and new logical values, reason, evidence, decision owner, and
first corrected release, and marks superseded assertions without erasing their
history. `release_changes` classifies rows as added, changed, superseded,
unchanged, removed from scope, or unresolved.

A refresh rechecks scope, cutoffs, dictionary compatibility, inaccessible
sources, document versions, URL events, aliases, entity relationships, and all
affected validation gates. Publish only the selected paired files and approved
documentation through the configured destination, then retain its publication
receipt and readback result separately from dataset acceptance.
